WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
Transitioning your data persistence layer from native Android to react native means mapping

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.280
the architectures you already know to their exact Expo module equivalence.

3
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:14.240
There's a common assumption that moving to cross-platform development forces you to

4
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.120
rely on slow web-based abstractions, like local storage.

5
00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.560
That is incorrect.

6
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.080
Expo modules act as a JavaScript interface.

7
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.040
They bind directly to the exact same native Android storage engines you are already using.

8
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.400
This table breaks down data persistence into five categories.

9
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.320
Key value, relational, internal files, user-picked documents, and secure data.

10
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.360
Establishing a baseline, this first row maps Android's shared preferences directly to

11
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.840
MMKV or async storage.

12
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.760
While MMKV is highly efficient for managing basic application state, the rest of your architecture

13
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.680
requires much deeper native integration.

14
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.040
Handling relational data, managing raw files, and cryptographic keys demands a different

15
00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.360
approach.

16
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.240
Having to a cross-platform framework does not mean giving up native performance.

17
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:06.960
It requires understanding the strict one-to-one mapping between existing native silos and their

18
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.480
respective Expo bridges.

19
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.280
Complex applications require a robust structure for persisting relational data.

20
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.480
You need a system that supports complex queries and strict data integrity.

21
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:24.560
Returning to our matrix, we map the Android standard, room, directly to the Expo SQL

22
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.000
Lite module.

23
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.080
This diagram illustrates an important distinction.

24
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:34.680
Expo SQL Lite is not a web SQL polyfill or a restricted browser sandbox.

25
00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.000
It bypasses those layers entirely to bind with the exact same native Android database engine

26
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.280
that powers room, operating asynchronously so the main thread remains unblocked.

27
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.680
The syntax design maintains strict feature parity.

28
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.280
Creating schemas in Expo SQL Lite directly mirrors the structure you define using rooms

29
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.960
database annotations.

30
00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:02.240
Furthermore, executing complex read, write, and update operations safely relies on SQL transactions

31
00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.920
that perfectly replicate rooms transaction annotations.

32
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.880
For modern architecture, this native database integrates seamlessly with state management tools

33
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.560
like React Query.

34
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.520
When you write relational queries and react native, you command the exact same native Android

35
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.920
database engine as in an application built entirely in Kotlin.

36
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.280
Having raw files introduces another layer of complexity.

37
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.640
The Android operating system splits file handling into two distinct permission paradigms and react

38
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.000
native maps to each separately.

39
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.600
Back on our matrix, we map internal arbitrary read and write operations, downloads, and directory

40
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.560
listings directly to Expo file system.

41
00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.840
This flow chart isolates the Expo file system module entirely within Branch A, built strictly

42
00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:51.160
for internal file caching and background management without user interaction.

43
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:57.920
We move to Branch B when a workflow requires user to manually select a document or media file.

44
00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:03.240
Because this interaction breaches the app internal boundary, React Native needs a dedicated module

45
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:08.400
designed specifically to trigger the native Android file picker user interface.

46
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.440
To execute this, the Expo document picker module binds directly to Android's intent for

47
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.680
opening documents and the system's storage access framework.

48
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.640
Native respects Android's compartmentalized boundaries by demanding one module for silent

49
00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:28.600
background operations and entirely different tooling for user prompted media selection.

50
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:33.920
Securing highly sensitive data requires moving past software-based encryption and locking cryptographic

51
00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.840
keys directly into the physical hardware of the device itself.

52
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.840
You must implement this strict security when storing authentication tokens or generating

53
00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.680
cryptographic signatures.

54
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:50.600
Speaking of the final layer of our storage matrix, Android's encrypted shared preferences

55
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:57.600
translates to either an encrypted MMKV instance or the Expo secure store module.

56
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.400
This architecture diagram shows how Expo secure store bypasses JavaScript-based encryption

57
00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:04.400
entirely.

58
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:10.920
Instead, the module routes directly to the native Android key generator and key store components.

59
00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:16.440
By leveraging these native systems, the resulting payload is encrypted using the advanced encryption

60
00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:20.080
standard with a 256-bit key length.

61
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.120
Because the module delegates the cryptographic heavylifting directly to the Android key store,

62
00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.920
the resulting encrypted data becomes permanently tethered to that specific device's physical

63
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:31.080
hardware.

64
00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:36.480
The Expo secure store module provides zero compromise on device security, replicating the

65
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.120
protections of native Android encrypted shared preferences, byte for byte.

66
00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.960
This completed table shows the full architectural mapping.

67
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.800
Transitioning from native Android to re-oct native does not demand that you discard your knowledge

68
00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.000
and learn entirely new storage paradigms.

69
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.200
Expo modules are explicitly designed to act as one-to-one bridges, connecting JavaScript

70
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:03.200
to the SQLite engine, the storage access framework, and the hardware key store.

71
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:08.640
This exact same cross-platform bridging logic extends well beyond data persistence.

72
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:14.240
Hardware sensors and device APIs like Android's sensor manager and location manager map just

73
00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.400
as directly to their JavaScript equivalents.

74
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.960
Mastering React Native architecture is an exercise in leveraging your existing native Android

75
00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.720
knowledge, simply executing it through a new declarative syntax.

